) to the <s>dharmabhāṇaka</s>, he attains the supreme <s>jñāna</s> (knowledge)-flowers; having given food and drink to the <s>dharmabhāṇaka</s>, the <s>bodhisattva</s> obtains the supreme nourishment of the <s>Tathāgata</s>; having covered the <s>dharmabhāṇaka</s> with a garment, he obtains the supreme complexion of the <s>Tathāgata</s>; and whoever covers the <s>dharmabhāṇaka</s> with jewels will also before long obtain the jewels of the thirty-seven <s>bodhipākṣika dharmas</s> (factors conducive to awakening).
Thus, O son of good family, this <s>Sarvajñatākāra-dhāraṇī-mukha-praveśa</s> is of great benefit to <s>bodhisattvas</s> and <s>mahāsattvas</s>. For what reason? Because herein the <s>bodhisattva-piṭaka</s> (collection of bodhisattva teachings) is taught in its entirety. And through this <s>Sarvajñatākāra-dhāraṇī-mukha-praveśa</s>, the <s>bodhisattva mahāsattva</s> attains unobstructed eloquence and attains the fourfold <s>dharma</s> of pleasing speech.
With these <s>Sarvajñatākāra-dhāraṇī-mukha-praveśas</s>, O son of good family, the <s>Tathāgata</s> <s>Candrottama</s>, the <s>Arhat</s>, the Perfectly Awakened One, when he addressed the <s>bodhisattva mahāsattva</s> <s>Gaganamudra</s>, there was likewise a great earthquake, and a great radiance appeared in the world, and <s>buddhakṣetras</s> beyond calculation in the ten directions were pervaded with magnificent brilliance. Likewise, the even and uneven, palm-flat ground of the earth was seen. And those <s>bodhisattvas</s> who had assembled there, in <s>buddhakṣetras</s> beyond calculation in the ten directions (
